        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Almost any product will make a basement crack look sealed. Very few will keep it sealed when groundwater pushes on the outside of the wall. The difference is depth: a surface caulk or coating sits on the inside face of the wall, while the water is coming through the full thickness of the concrete. If a crack leaks, the sealer has to fill the crack from front to back or be backed by a pressure-rated plug. If it is dry and stable, a surface sealer may be all it ever needs. Choosing the right basement crack sealer starts with being honest about which crack you have.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="diagnose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Before You Buy a Basement Crack Sealer, Diagnose the Crack
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Four Questions That Decide It</h3>

          <p>
            Four questions decide which basement wall crack sealer fits. Does it leak, and if so only after heavy rain or constantly? Is it still moving, meaning it gets wider over months or opens and closes with the seasons? Is it in a poured concrete wall, a block wall or the floor slab? And are there signs the wall itself is failing, such as a horizontal crack, a bow, or one side of the crack offset from the other? Write the answers down before you shop. They narrow five product types to one or two.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Confirm a Leak</h3>

          <p>
            Leaking is easy to confirm. Look for efflorescence, the white mineral crust that water leaves as it evaporates, along the crack line. Look for rust-colored staining or damp concrete after a storm. If you are unsure, tape a square of plastic sheet over the crack for 48 hours: moisture under the plastic means water through the wall, moisture on top means humid basement air condensing. Movement is harder. Mark the ends of the crack with a pencil and a date, and measure its width at a fixed point every couple of months.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Poured Walls: Usually Shrinkage Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Most poured-wall cracks are vertical or slightly diagonal shrinkage cracks, often starting at a window corner or tie hole, under 1/8 in wide and not structural. They leak because water finds the path of least resistance, not because the wall is failing. These are exactly the cracks basement crack sealers are designed for. Horizontal cracks, stair-step cracks in block, bowing, and cracks wider than about 1/4 in with displacement are different: sealing them hides a symptom. Our guide to <a href="/concrete-wall-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete wall crack repair</a> covers those warning signs in depth.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Block Walls Are Different</h3>

          <p>
            Block walls are also a different animal. Hollow concrete block has cores that fill with water, and a crack in a block wall rarely has a continuous void that injected material can fill. Injection is a poured-wall technique. Block walls are usually handled with drainage and surface coatings, and floor cracks are covered in our <a href="/basement-floor-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement floor crack repair</a> guide.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
