        <div id="poly-injection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Polyurethane Injection: The Default for Leaking Poured Walls
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Polyurethane Is the Default</h3>

          <p>
            For a leaking crack in a poured concrete wall, low-pressure polyurethane injection is the method most foundation contractors reach for, and it is the backbone of most DIY injection kits. The resin is pumped into the crack through small surface ports, travels through the full thickness of the wall, reacts with moisture and expands into a flexible foam or gel that seals the crack from the inside out. Because it stays flexible after cure, it tolerates the small seasonal movement that most foundation cracks have.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Hydrophobic vs Hydrophilic</h3>

          <p>
            There are two broad chemistries. Hydrophobic polyurethane foams repel water once cured and hold their volume whether the crack is wet or dry. Hydrophilic resins absorb water and swell, and some can shrink back if the crack dries out for long periods and swell again when it gets wet. Manufacturers typically recommend hydrophobic resins for cracks that see wet and dry cycles and hydrophilic ones for cracks that stay damp. For a homeowner kit, the product chooses for you, so read which kind it is.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Works on Wet Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            The big advantage is that polyurethane works in the presence of water. Many products are designed to be injected into a crack that is damp or even weeping, and the water itself triggers the reaction. That makes it the right basement crack sealer when you cannot wait for dry weather. The trade-off is that polyurethane adds no structural strength. It seals; it does not glue the wall back together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Effort Involved</h3>

          <p>
            The other trade-off is effort. Proper injection means grinding the crack face clean, setting ports every 6 to 12 in along it, covering the crack between ports with an epoxy paste that has to cure before you inject, then pumping each port until resin appears at the next. Kits typically cover a crack of about 8 to 10 ft. Our <a href="/concrete-repair-kit/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">concrete repair kit guide</a> explains what comes in the box and what you will still need to buy.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">General Reference Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">6-12 in</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical port spacing</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">8-10 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Crack length many kits cover</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">Flexible</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Polyurethane after cure</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Wet OK</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most PU resins tolerate damp cracks</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="epoxy-injection" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Epoxy Injection: For Dry Cracks That Need Strength
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How Epoxy Injection Works</h3>

          <p>
            Epoxy injection uses the same port-and-paste method, but the material is a low-viscosity two-part epoxy that cures rigid and bonds the two faces of the crack together. Manufacturers rate structural injection epoxies to be stronger than the surrounding concrete, which is why engineers specify it when a crack has compromised a wall and the cause has already been fixed. Done properly, it restores the wall to something close to one piece.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">It Needs a Dry, Stable Crack</h3>

          <p>
            The catch is that epoxy needs a crack that is dry or nearly dry and no longer moving. Water in the crack weakens the bond and can stop the epoxy from filling the void. And because cured epoxy is rigid, a crack that keeps moving will simply crack again beside the epoxy line, often with a new leak. That is why injecting epoxy into a seasonally active shrinkage crack is usually a mistake: you end up with a stronger wall that still leaks.
          </p>

          <p>
            Epoxy is also slower and fussier to inject. It flows more slowly than polyurethane, takes longer to travel through a wall, and many products need hours to cure, so injection is done patiently, port by port, sometimes refilling ports as the resin soaks in. Cold basements slow it further. Labels commonly specify minimum temperatures in the 40s or 50s F for both the paste and the injection resin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Epoxy Is the Right Choice</h3>

          <p>
            So when is epoxy the right basement crack sealer? When the crack is dry, it is not getting wider, and there is a structural reason to rebond it, typically after an engineer has looked at it. For the common leaking shrinkage crack, polyurethane is the better fit. Our <a href="/foundation-crack-repair-cost/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">foundation crack repair cost</a> guide covers what contractors typically charge for each type of injection.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Polyurethane for water, epoxy for strength.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If the crack leaks and moves a little, inject polyurethane. If it is dry, stable and structurally important, inject epoxy. If it leaks and is structural, fix the cause first, then talk to an engineer.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="hydraulic-cement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Hydraulic Cement: The Emergency Plug, Not the Permanent Seal
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What Hydraulic Cement Does Well</h3>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement is a fast-setting cement that expands slightly as it cures and sets in a few minutes, even under running water. Pressed into a gushing crack or tie hole, it can stop flowing water when nothing else will stay in place long enough to cure. That makes it uniquely useful for active, pressurized leaks, and it is the reason it sits on so many basement shelves.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why It Fails in Moving Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            Its weakness is that it is rigid and bonds mechanically rather than chemically. In a crack that opens and closes with the seasons, a hydraulic cement plug tends to crack along its edges within a year or two, and the leak returns beside the patch. It also only fills the outer inch or two of the crack. Water still travels through the wall behind the plug and can find a new way out a few inches away.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Use It on a Crack</h3>

          <p>
            The right way to use it for a crack is to chisel a V-groove or, better, an undercut groove about 3/4 in wide and deep along the crack so the plug locks in, wet the groove, then press the cement in firmly and hold it while it sets. Our guide on <a href="/how-to-use-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">how to use hydraulic cement</a> covers the technique and the short working window, and our <a href="/best-hydraulic-cement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hydraulic cement roundup</a> compares products.
          </p>

          <p>
            Think of hydraulic cement as the tourniquet. It stops the immediate flow so you can dry the area, then inject the crack properly or apply a coating over it. For the gap where the floor meets the wall, a common leak point, it is often part of the fix but rarely the whole fix; see our guide to a <a href="/basement-leaking-where-floor-meets-wall/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement leaking where floor meets wall</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="caulk" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Polyurethane Caulk and Surface Sealants: Fine for Dry Cracks Only
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where Caulk Works</h3>

          <p>
            The most common basement crack sealer sold at hardware stores is a tube of polyurethane or hybrid polymer concrete caulk. It is cheap, easy and flexible, and on a dry, stable crack it is a perfectly reasonable choice. It keeps dust and radon from finding an easy path through the crack, blocks insects, and gives a clean line if you plan to paint or coat the wall.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why Caulk Fails Against Water Pressure</h3>

          <p>
            What it cannot do is resist water pressure from the outside. A caulk bead sits in the first 1/4 to 1/2 in of the crack on the interior side. Groundwater coming through the wall pushes on the back of that bead, and since the bead only bonds to a sliver of concrete, the water either pushes it off or travels sideways and comes out beside it. Owners commonly report that caulked leaking cracks are dry for a season and then weep again along the edges.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How to Caulk a Dry Crack</h3>

          <p>
            If you do caulk, prep the same way you would for injection: grind or wire-brush the crack face, vacuum it, and let it dry. For cracks over 1/4 in wide, push in backer rod so the bead is a manageable depth. Our article on <a href="/caulk-for-basement-wall-cracks/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">caulk for basement wall cracks</a> compares caulk chemistries and explains when caulk is the right call.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid silicone and acrylic latex for this job. Silicone does not bond well to masonry over the long term and nothing sticks to it afterward, so you cannot coat over it. Acrylic latex shrinks and hardens. Neither is rated for the alkaline, damp environment of a foundation wall.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="coatings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Membranes, Coatings and Crystalline Sealers
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Whole-Wall Products</h3>

          <p>
            The last group of basement wall crack sealers works across the whole wall rather than in the crack. Masonry waterproofing paints, elastomeric coatings, crystalline waterproofing products and peel-and-stick or fabric-reinforced membranes all form or create a barrier on the interior face. On walls with general dampness or seepage through porous concrete or block, a coating can make a real difference. Our <a href="/best-basement-wall-sealer/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement wall sealer guide</a> ranks the main products.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Interior Coatings Are on the Wrong Side</h3>

          <p>
            Over a crack, though, a coating has the same problem as caulk: it is on the wrong side of the wall. Interior coatings are called negative-side waterproofing because water pushes them away from the wall, not into it. Many masonry waterproofers are rated to resist significant hydrostatic pressure on sound concrete, but a crack concentrates water at one line and can open slightly with the seasons, which a rigid paint film cannot bridge. Most manufacturers say to seal cracks first, then coat.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Elastomeric and Crystalline Options</h3>

          <p>
            Elastomeric and fabric-reinforced systems can bridge small moving cracks better than rigid paints, because the coating stretches. Crystalline products take a different approach: they react with moisture and lime in the concrete to grow crystals in the pores and fine cracks, and manufacturers rate them for hairline cracks. They are not designed to close a crack you can fit a coin into.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exterior Membranes</h3>

          <p>
            Exterior membranes are the opposite case. Applied to the outside of the foundation during excavation, they are positive-side waterproofing and are the most reliable way to stop water through a wall. They are also by far the most expensive, because the dirt has to come out. That is usually reserved for walls with multiple leaking cracks, or where interior work has failed repeatedly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="decide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Choosing and Applying the Right Basement Crack Sealer
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">A Leaking Crack in a Poured Wall</h3>

          <p>
            For a single leaking vertical crack in a poured wall, the order is simple. Fix the outside first if you can: extend downspouts at least 4 to 6 ft from the foundation and check that soil slopes away from the house. Then inject polyurethane. If water is actively pouring through, plug it with hydraulic cement first, let it set, and inject through ports set beside the plug. Coat afterward only if the rest of the wall is damp.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dry Hairlines and Cracks That Need a Pro</h3>

          <p>
            For a dry hairline crack that has not changed in a year, caulk it or coat over it, or simply leave it and keep monitoring. For a crack wider than about 1/4 in, a horizontal crack, a stair-step crack in block, or any crack where one side has shifted, stop and get the wall assessed before sealing anything. A sealer on a moving structural crack just hides the evidence an engineer would use to diagnose it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Prep Is Always the Same</h3>

          <p>
            Whatever you choose, the prep is the same. Remove paint, efflorescence and loose material from at least an inch either side of the crack with a wire wheel or grinder. Vacuum the crack. Dry it unless the product says damp is fine. Respect the label minimum temperature, which many sealers put around 40 F to 50 F, and remember that a basement wall in early spring can be colder than the air in the room.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Test the Repair Properly</h3>

          <p>
            Then judge the result after a real test. One heavy storm, or a garden hose soaking the soil outside the crack for half an hour, tells you more than a week of dry weather. If the crack still weeps, reinject that section rather than adding more material on the surface. And if water is rising through the floor or coming in at many points, a crack sealer is the wrong tool entirely; read our guides to <a href="/hydrostatic-pressure-in-basement/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">hydrostatic pressure in a basement</a> and <a href="/basement-crack-repair/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-semibold hover:underline">basement crack repair</a> for the drainage side of the problem.
          </p>

