        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best basement crack sealer for a leaking wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a leaking crack in a poured concrete wall, low-pressure polyurethane injection is generally the best choice. The resin travels through the full thickness of the wall, reacts with moisture, and expands into a flexible seal that tolerates seasonal movement. It can usually be injected into a damp crack. Surface caulks and coatings rarely stop a pressurized leak on their own.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is epoxy or polyurethane better for basement wall cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on the job. Polyurethane is better for sealing leaks, because it stays flexible and tolerates moisture during injection. Epoxy is better when a dry, stable crack needs structural strength restored, because it bonds the two faces together rigidly. Injecting epoxy into a crack that still moves or is wet usually leads to a new crack and a returning leak.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I seal a basement crack from the inside?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Injection from the inside is the standard method for leaking poured wall cracks, because the resin is pushed through the wall to the outside face. Interior caulks and coatings also work for dry cracks. What interior surface products cannot reliably do is hold back water pressure on their own. For multiple leaks, exterior waterproofing is the most reliable fix.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does hydraulic cement permanently fix a leaking crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually not. Hydraulic cement sets in minutes and can stop flowing water, but it is rigid and only fills the outer part of the crack. In a crack that moves seasonally, owners commonly report the plug cracking along its edges and the leak returning. Use it to stop an active leak, then inject the crack or coat the wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will Drylok or other waterproofing paint seal a crack?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Masonry waterproofing paints are designed for porous walls with general dampness, not for open cracks. Most manufacturers instruct you to fill or seal cracks before painting. A paint film over a moving crack will split along the crack line, and water concentrated there can push the coating off. Seal the crack first, then paint the wall if seepage is widespread.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I use basement crack injection on a block wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. Injection works by filling a continuous crack through solid concrete. Hollow concrete block has open cores, so injected resin flows into the cores instead of sealing the crack. Block wall leaks are usually handled with exterior drainage, interior drainage systems, and masonry coatings. Stair-step cracks in block also need a structural assessment before any repair.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know if a basement crack needs a professional?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Call a professional if the crack is horizontal, forms a stair-step pattern in block, is wider than about 1/4 in, shows one side offset from the other, or comes with a bowing or leaning wall. Also call if the crack keeps widening when you measure it over months. Those signs point to soil pressure or settlement, not simple shrinkage.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Caulking a crack that leaks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A caulk bead sits in the first fraction of an inch of the crack, on the side water pushes away from. Groundwater travels through the wall behind it and either lifts it off or exits beside it. Caulk dry, stable cracks. For a leaking crack, the seal has to run through the full wall thickness.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using epoxy on an active crack</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Epoxy cures rigid. If the crack still opens and closes with the seasons, or is wet when injected, the epoxy line holds while the concrete beside it cracks again, often bringing the leak back. Use flexible polyurethane for active leaks, and save epoxy for dry, stable, structurally important cracks.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating hydraulic cement as permanent</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Hydraulic cement stops flowing water fast, but it is rigid and only fills the outer inch or two. In a moving crack it typically cracks along its edges, and water behind it finds a new exit. Use it to stop an active leak, then follow up with injection or a coating.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Coating over cracks without sealing them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Masonry paints and many coatings cannot bridge a crack that moves, and water concentrated at the crack line pushes on the film from behind. Most manufacturers say to seal or inject cracks first and coat second. Skipping that step leaves blisters and peeling right along the crack.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing a structural crack without an assessment</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Horizontal cracks, stair-step cracks in block, bowing walls and cracks wider than 1/4 in with offset are signs of soil pressure or settlement. Sealing them hides the evidence and does nothing about the cause. Get an engineer or reputable foundation contractor to look before any sealer goes in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

